//? Title: Branch and Bound: Exact Knapsack Search with Visible Pruning
//? Subtitle: Integer-safe fractional bounds, real DFS records and the limits of a favourable experiment
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A 0/1 knapsack solver chooses whole items to maximise value within a weight
budget. Branch and bound explores take/skip decisions while discarding branches
whose optimistic completion cannot improve the best feasible selection already
found. We inspect five actual visits on a small instance, preserve the original
five-item problem and seeded scaling experiment, and add a family whose loose
bound keeps many branches alive. The core computes exact density comparisons,
rational relaxation bounds and their integer floors; the report reads actual
incumbents, decisions and pruning records from that same solver.
)

\toc

> Small example: five visits prove value eighteen

Take values {10,8,5}, weights {2,3,3} and capacity five. Their decreasing
value densities already give the input order. Taking items zero and one yields
weight five and value eighteen. A partial selection is feasible even before
all remaining decisions are made, since every undecided item can be skipped;
it can therefore improve the incumbent immediately.

The diagram contains every actual visited node. After finding eighteen, the
solver prunes the branch that takes item zero but skips item one: its best
remaining completion is only fifteen. Skipping item zero has fractional bound
8+5(2/3)=11 1/3, whose integer floor eleven also cannot improve eighteen.
The node that already achieves eighteen is itself bound-pruned: remaining
capacity is zero, so no better completion exists.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knapsack_small_tree.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::knapsack_branch_and_bound_trace({10,8,5},{2,3,3},5);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{0,0},{6,2.5},{12,5},{12,0},{6,-3.5}};
  if (trace.nodes.size()!=position.size()) return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.nodes.size();++i) {
    const auto& node=trace.nodes[i];dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.8;style.height=2.2;
    const auto state=node.state==dm::algorithms::KnapsackNodeState::branch?"branch":
        node.state==dm::algorithms::KnapsackNodeState::pruned?"prune":"leaf";
    style.fill={219,234,254};
    if (node.state==dm::algorithms::KnapsackNodeState::pruned) style.fill={254,226,226};
    if (node.incumbent_after>node.incumbent_before) style.fill={220,252,231};
    diagram.node(std::format("n{}",i),position[i],std::format("node {}: level {}\nw {}, v {}\nupper {}\nbest {} -> {}\n{}",
        i,node.level,node.weight,node.value,node.bound.integer_upper,node.incumbent_before,node.incumbent_after,state),style);
  }
  for (std::size_t i=1;i<trace.nodes.size();++i) {
    const auto& node=trace.nodes[i];
    diagram.edge(std::format("n{}",node.parent),std::format("n{}",i),
        std::format("{} item {}",node.took_item?"take":"skip",node.item_index));
  }
  diagram.text({6,-6},"Green: incumbent improved. Red: bound prune. Unvisited descendants are omitted.")
      .save_svg("build/knapsack_small_tree.svg");
  const auto& bound=trace.nodes.back().bound;
  std::print("| optimum | original-index choices | visited nodes | bound prunes | leaves |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
      trace.result.value,trace.result.chosen,trace.result.nodes_explored,trace.bound_prunes,trace.leaves);
  std::print("\nLast branch relaxation: {} + {}/{}; integer upper bound {}.\n",
      bound.whole_value,bound.fractional_numerator,bound.fractional_denominator,bound.integer_upper);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| optimum | original-index choices | visited nodes | bound prunes | leaves |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 18 | [1, 1, 0] | 5 | 3 | 0 |

Last branch relaxation: 8 + 10/3; integer upper bound 11.
\image(figures/knapsack_small_tree.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All five nodes come from the canonical take-first DFS. Parent links supply the arrows; integer upper bounds, incumbent updates and terminal states are actual records.)
\alttext(The root branches on item zero. Taking it branches on item one and finds value eighteen. That winning node, its sibling of upper bound fifteen, and the root's skip branch of upper bound eleven are all pruned.)

> Why the bound preserves the optimum

For item values vi, weights wi and capacity C, solve

$$
\max\sum_i v_i z_i,\qquad \sum_i w_i z_i\le C,\qquad z_i\in\{0,1\}.
$$
\alttext(Maximise the sum of selected item values, subject to total selected weight no greater than capacity and binary item decisions.)

The empty selection is allowed. With nonnegative weights, items of nonpositive
value never improve the optimum and are omitted from the active search. Free
positive-value items come first. Other active items are sorted by value/weight,
using exact widened cross-products and original-index tie order.

At a feasible node of value V and remaining capacity R, relax undecided
choices to fractions between zero and one. Greedily fill in density order:
include whole items while they fit, then use a fraction of the next item.
If W is the value after the whole additions, and the final item has value v
and positive weight w, the bound is B=W+vR/w. Every integral completion is
also a relaxed completion, so its value is at most B. Integer profits permit
the stronger safe upper bound U=floor(B).

The core stores W, vR and w exactly and computes U with integer division.
No floating-point value controls density order or pruning. A rounded LP value
is available for display. At each visit, the current feasible partial selection
first improves the incumbent if strictly better. If U is no greater than that
incumbent, there is no improving descendant, so the node is pruned. Otherwise
try taking the next item if it fits, then skipping it. Equal optima retain the
first solution found under this deterministic order.

> Original five-item instance

Retain values {60,100,120,40,70}, weights {10,20,30,15,25} and capacity
fifty. Items one and two form the optimum: value 100+120=220 and weight
20+30=50. The selected bits stay in original input order even though the
search processes items by density. The program measures node visits and pruning
states from the exact integer-bound kernel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knapsack_incumbent.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<int> values{60,100,120,40,70},weights{10,20,30,15,25};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::knapsack_branch_and_bound_trace(values,weights,50);
  const auto plain=dm::algorithms::knapsack_branch_and_bound(values,weights,50);
  if (trace.result.value!=plain.value || trace.result.chosen!=plain.chosen || trace.result.nodes_explored!=plain.nodes_explored) return 1;
  std::print("| item | value | weight | selected |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<values.size();++i) std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,values[i],weights[i],trace.result.chosen[i]);
  std::print("\nDensity order: {}. Optimum: {}. Visited nodes: {}; bound prunes: {}; infeasible takes: {}.\n",
      trace.item_order,trace.result.value,trace.result.nodes_explored,trace.bound_prunes,trace.infeasible_takes);
  std::vector<double> visit,best,prune_visit,prune_best;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.nodes.size();++i) {
    const auto& node=trace.nodes[i];visit.push_back(i+1);best.push_back(node.incumbent_after);
    if (node.state==dm::algorithms::KnapsackNodeState::pruned) {
      prune_visit.push_back(i+1);prune_best.push_back(node.incumbent_after);
    }
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(visit,best,"incumbent",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2)
      .points(prune_visit,prune_best,"bound prune",dm::RGB{220,38,38},4)
      .title("Original five-item knapsack: incumbent by actual visit")
      .x_label("node visit, starting at one").y_label("best feasible value after entry")
      .y_limits(0,300).size(900,480).save_svg("build/knapsack_incumbent.svg");
}
```
