> Limits: a parity gap keeps branches alive

A good result on the seeded inputs is not a guarantee for arbitrary instances.
For even n, give every item value two and weight two, and set capacity n-1.
The integral optimum is n-2 because feasible values are even. The root's
fractional bound is n-1, and flooring it still gives n-1. It cannot prove the
extra parity restriction, so many branches remain promising after the true
optimum has already been found. The following table shows the resulting growth.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <limits>
int main() {
  std::print("| n | capacity | optimum | theoretical root upper | actual visits | unrestricted tree |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{4,6,8,10,12,14,16,18}) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::knapsack_branch_and_bound(std::vector<int>(n,2),std::vector<int>(n,2),n-1);
    if (result.value!=n-2) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,n-1,result.value,n-1,result.nodes_explored,(std::uint64_t{1}<<(n+1))-1);
  }
  struct Example {std::string name;std::vector<int> values,weights;int capacity;};
  const int maximum=std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
  const std::vector<Example> examples{{"empty",{},{},0},{"optional negative profit",{1,-100},{1,1},2},
    {"free positive item",{7,0,-9,4},{0,0,0,2},0},{"zero capacity",{5},{1},0},
    {"large weight boundary",{1,1},{maximum,maximum},maximum}};
  std::print("\n| control | optimum | original-index choices | visited nodes |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& example:examples) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::knapsack_branch_and_bound(example.values,example.weights,example.capacity);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",example.name,result.value,result.chosen,result.nodes_explored);
  }
}
```
