// result_end
\caption(The parity family demonstrates a loose relaxation without requiring a large input. Controls also exercise the signed-profit policy, free items and overflow-safe weight feasibility.)

The solver requires equal-length input vectors, nonnegative weights and
nonnegative capacity. It rejects invalid inputs before indexing. Profits can
be signed, with nonpositive items left unchosen. A feasible value above the
legacy int result range proves that the optimum is unrepresentable, so the
call raises overflow_error; exact bound sums and visit counts are checked too.
Weight feasibility uses remaining capacity, avoiding overflow in tentative sums.

With k active positive-profit items and N visits, preprocessing costs
O(n+k log k), and bound scans and incumbent copies can cost O(kN). N remains
exponential in the worst case. The ordinary solver uses O(n+k) storage;
its explicit stack avoids C++ recursion-depth limits. Recording adds O(N+k)
storage for actual nodes, parent links and density order. Inputs are unchanged
and results own their data. Generated instances are appropriate here; no
observational dataset is needed to establish the search invariants.

> References

- A. H. Land and A. G. Doig (1960), An Automatic Method of Solving Discrete
  Programming Problems, Econometrica 28(3), 497–520.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.2307/1910129).
- P. J. Kolesar (1967), A Branch and Bound Algorithm for the Knapsack Problem,
  Management Science 13(9), 723–735.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.13.9.723).

The core uses an exact integer fractional bound with iterative take-first DFS;
this report does not reproduce every strategy or stopping rule in those papers.
